#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179959 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "976e05d4-59a8-5a93-be2f-b22357f388b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Circulation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "553c3284-1cc8-5cd6-bf53-43e787406d04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177755} {:answer/id #uuid "6492d3b1-fbd2-5113-a984-3816de854e5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177756} {:answer/id #uuid "67cdb085-05c1-5c1d-a6f0-ebab6a3d0019" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177757} {:answer/id #uuid "6e8ca0af-0e24-5ef6-be62-3bc3b0bcb776" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177758} {:answer/id #uuid "71e7bf95-6f9c-59a9-8bf1-fa297751aed2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177759}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "553c3284-1cc8-5cd6-bf53-43e787406d04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177755} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3dc50a4-262e-58bd-857d-bd66b3231b7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177754}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "81113ee4-2843-5316-a3de-8f3bdbd20c2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-125485" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y)=\\frac{5}{y}\\,i+\\frac{1}{x}\\,j$ along the ellipse $C$ with equation $25{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25$ oriented counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $1$ and $5,$ respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\frac{5}{y}\\,i+\\frac{1}{x}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-1+5 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4t\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =4(2\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =8\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
